window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"id": "start", "url": "start.html", "title": "Core ideas", "text": "overview core ideas six principles behind how azure behaves. most surprises trace back to one of them. cloud is renting, by the minute instead of buying servers, you ask azure for a database or a web host and it appears in minutes. you pay while it exists, so deleting what you no longer need matters as much as creating it. everything is a resource a vm, a database, a network, even a firewall rule set is a \"resource\" with a name, a region, a price tier (sku) and settings. resources live in resource groups, like folders. everything is an api call clicking \"create\" in the portal sends the same request the cli or a bicep file sends. that is why teams describe infrastructure in code: it is just the list of requests, saved and repeatable. identity replaces passwords apps prove who they are with identities from entra id (managed identities), not passwords stored in config. when something says \"access denied\", the first question is: which identity, which role, at which scope? managed means less to patch the higher up the stack you go (vm \u2192 app service \u2192 functions), the more azure handles for you and the less control you have. most new apps start at the paas layer. public by default, private by design many services start with a public endpoint on the internet. production setups usually lock them into a private network. much of an infrastructure job is that wiring. five habits worth keeping: put every experiment in its own resource group and delete the whole group when done \u00b7 set a budget alert on any subscription you use \u00b7 never paste keys or connection strings into code or chat \u00b7 run what-if before every deployment \u00b7 tag resources with owner and env ."}, {"id": "mental-model", "url": "mental-model.html", "title": "Mental model", "text": "chapter 1 the mental model almost every azure question gets easier once you know three things: where a resource sits in the hierarchy, which api you are talking to, and where it physically runs. the scope hierarchy permissions (rbac), policies, budgets and tags are assigned at a scope and inherit downward. scope 0 entra tenant your organization's identity directory (formerly azure ad). holds users, groups, app registrations. one tenant can own many subscriptions. think: \"who are you?\" scope 1 management groups folders for subscriptions, up to 6 levels deep under the tenant root group. assign org-wide policy and rbac here (e.g. \"corp\", \"online\", \"sandbox\"). think: governance at scale scope 2 subscriptions the billing and quota boundary. each has its own limits and invoice line. teams commonly get one subscription per app per environment. think: \"who pays, and what are the limits?\" scope 3 resource groups a lifecycle container. resources that are deployed, updated and deleted together live in one group. a group has a region for its metadata, but its resources can live anywhere. think: \"what gets deleted together?\" scope 4 resources the actual things: a vm, a storage account, a sql database. each has a resource id like /subscriptions/{id}/resourcegroups/{rg}/providers/microsoft.storage/storageaccounts/{name} . think: the unit you pay for control plane vs data plane every create, update or delete goes through azure resource manager (arm) at management.azure.com , whether you use the portal, cli, bicep or terraform. that is the control plane. reading a blob or querying a database hits the service's own endpoint, e.g. myacct.blob.core.windows.net . that is the data plane, and it often has separate permissions. common trap: \"owner\" on a storage account lets you manage it but does not grant reading blobs through entra auth. you need a data role such as storage blob data reader . regions, zones, pairs region: a set of datacenters in a geography (e.g. eastus2 , westeurope ). not every service or vm size exists in every region. availability zone: physically separate datacenters inside a region with independent power and cooling. zone-redundant deployment survives one datacenter failing. region pair: two regions in the same geography used for sequenced platform updates and geo-replication (e.g. east us 2 \u2194 central us). some newer regions have no pair. sovereign clouds: azure government and azure operated by 21vianet (china) are separate clouds with separate endpoints. resource providers services are exposed by namespaces like microsoft.compute , microsoft.web , microsoft.network . a subscription must have a provider registered before you can create its resources. each resource type has versioned apis ( 2023-05-01 style). bicep and arm templates pin those versions, which is why templates keep working when the service changes. az provider register -n microsoft.app the shared-responsibility split model you manage azure manages examples iaas os patching, runtime, app, data, network rules hardware, hypervisor, physical network virtual machines, vm scale sets, managed disks paas app code, data, configuration, access os, runtime patching, scaling machinery app service, azure sql database, container apps serverless function code, triggers, bindings everything else, including scale to zero functions (flex consumption), logic apps, event grid saas users, data, settings the whole application microsoft 365, dynamics 365, fabric"}, {"id": "identity", "url": "identity.html", "title": "Identity & governance", "text": "chapter 2 identity & governance identity is the real perimeter in azure. most incidents and most \"access denied\" tickets come down to who can do what, at which scope. who: security principals user : a person in entra id (or a guest via b2b). group : assign roles to groups, not individuals. service principal : an app's identity in a tenant, created from an app registration . authenticates with a secret, certificate or federated credential. managed identity : a service principal azure manages for you, with no secrets to rotate. system-assigned lives and dies with one resource; user-assigned is a standalone resource you can attach to many. workload identity federation : lets github actions, kubernetes or another cloud get entra tokens via oidc with no stored secret. what: azure rbac a role assignment = principal + role definition + scope. owner : everything, including granting access. contributor : manage everything, but cannot grant access. reader : view only. user access administrator / role based access control administrator : manage role assignments only. hundreds of service-specific roles (e.g. key vault secrets user , acrpull ). deny assignments exist but are created by the platform (e.g. deployment stacks, managed apps), not by you directly. entra roles \u2260 azure roles entra roles (global administrator, application administrator, user administrator) control the directory: users, apps, licenses. azure rbac roles control resources in subscriptions. a global admin has no azure resource access by default, though they can elevate themselves to user access administrator at root scope. hardening access conditional access : rules like \"require mfa and a compliant device to reach the azure portal\". privileged identity management (pim) : make admin roles eligible, then activate just-in-time with approval and a time limit. access reviews : periodic recertification of group and role membership. break-glass accounts : two cloud-only emergency admins excluded from most ca policies, closely monitored. azure policy rules evaluated against resource properties. assign a single definition or a bundle ( initiative ) at any scope. effects: deny , audit , modify , append , deployifnotexists , auditifnotexists , denyaction , disabled . typical uses: allowed regions, required tags, no public ips, enforce diagnostic settings, enforce private endpoints. other guardrails tags : key/value metadata ( env , owner , costcenter ). not inherited unless a policy copies them. resource locks : cannotdelete or readonly , applied even to owners. cost management + budgets : alerts at thresholds; cost analysis by tag, resource group or service. deployment stacks : manage a set of resources as one unit, with deny settings to block out-of-band changes. the successor to azure blueprints. azure landing zones : microsoft's reference management-group and policy layout from the cloud adoption framework."}, {"id": "catalog", "url": "catalog.html", "title": "Service catalog", "text": "chapter 3 service catalog the services you are most likely to meet, with what each is for and its key concepts. the tier badge says how often you will run into it: core nearly every environment, common frequently, specialist specific workloads, retiring plan a migration. no services match. try a broader word, or clear the category filter."}, {"id": "choose", "url": "choose.html", "title": "Which one do I pick?", "text": "chapter 4 which one do i pick? azure often has three or four services that look alike. these tables cover the comparisons that come up most in design reviews. compute messaging load balancing databases private connectivity storage redundancy service pick it when you manage scale to zero watch out for virtual machines lift-and-shift, custom os, licensed software, full control os, patching, runtime no (deallocate to stop compute billing) you own patching and backups; disks bill even when stopped app service web apps and apis in .net, java, node, python, php, or a container code + config no you pay for the plan, not per app; many apps can share one plan functions event-driven bits: a queue message, timer, http hook, blob upload function code yes (flex consumption / consumption) cold starts; execution time limits on consumption plans container apps containerized microservices and jobs without running kubernetes yourself container images + scale rules yes no direct kubernetes api access; check feature gaps vs aks aks you need the full kubernetes api, operators, service mesh, many teams on one platform workloads, node pools, upgrades, add-ons node pools can scale to 0 (user pools) real platform-team effort: upgrades, networking, security container instances run one container quickly, a batch task, or a ci agent container billed per second while running no built-in autoscale or load balancing batch large parallel hpc or rendering jobs across many vms job definitions, pools pools can scale to 0 job scheduling model takes some learning static web apps spa or static site with optional managed functions api front-end build n/a (free tier exists) the managed api is limited; link your own backend for more service shape pick it when key features not for service bus enterprise message broker (queues, topics) commands and business transactions that must not be lost: \"process order 123\" sessions (fifo per key), dead-lettering, duplicate detection, transactions, scheduled messages millions of telemetry events per second event grid push-based event router (pub/sub) reacting to something that happened: \"blob created\", \"resource deleted\" filters, retries, cloudevents schema, mqtt broker, system topics for azure services ordered processing, large payloads event hubs partitioned append-only log (kafka-compatible) high-volume streams: telemetry, clickstream, logs partitions, consumer groups, capture to storage, kafka protocol endpoint, retention/replay per-message acknowledgement workflows storage queues simple queue inside a storage account cheap, basic background work; very large backlogs huge capacity, http api, very low cost ordering, topics, dead-lettering (you build it) web pubsub / signalr real-time push to browsers/clients live dashboards, chat, notifications websockets at scale, managed connections server-to-server messaging service layer reach pick it when extras load balancer l4 (tcp/udp) regional distribute traffic to vms or scale sets; internal or public ha ports, outbound rules, zone-redundant frontends application gateway l7 (http/s) regional path-based routing, tls termination, waf in front of apps in a vnet waf v2, autoscale, aks ingress via application gateway for containers front door l7 (http/s) global edge global entry point, cdn caching, multi-region failover waf, private link to origins (premium), rules engine traffic manager dns global dns-level routing for any protocol (priority, weighted, geographic, performance) works for non-http; failover is bounded by dns ttl service model pick it when key concepts watch out for azure sql database relational (sql server engine), paas new relational apps, saas per-tenant dbs vcore vs dtu, serverless, hyperscale, elastic pools, geo-replication, failover groups no sql agent or cross-database queries in the usual way sql managed instance near-complete sql server instance, paas migrating existing sql server estates with agent jobs, clr, cross-db queries vnet-injected, instance pools, link to on-prem sql higher entry cost; deploys into a dedicated subnet postgresql / mysql flexible server open-source relational, paas open-source stacks, django/rails/laravel apps burstable/gp/memory tiers, zone-redundant ha, read replicas, extensions major-version upgrades need planning cosmos db multi-model nosql, globally distributed low-latency at global scale, flexible schema, multi-region writes request units (ru/s), partition keys, 5 consistency levels, change feed, vector search a bad partition key is very expensive to fix later azure managed redis in-memory key-value caching, sessions, rate-limiting, leaderboards eviction policies, clustering, persistence, active geo-replication azure cache for redis is retiring; new work should use managed redis data explorer / fabric eventhouse append-heavy analytics (kql) log and telemetry analytics at huge scale kql, ingestion mappings, materialized views not a transactional store option what it does traffic path use when private endpoint gives a paas resource a private ip in your subnet (private link) fully private; works from peered vnets and on-prem default choice for locking down paas. pair with private dns zones like privatelink.blob.core.windows.net . service endpoint extends the subnet's identity to a service so its firewall can allow that subnet optimized route, but the service keeps its public ip simple, free, same-region; does not cover on-prem traffic vnet integration lets app service/functions make outbound calls into a vnet outbound only your web app needs to reach a private database. combine with a private endpoint for inbound. vpn gateway encrypted ipsec tunnel over the internet site-to-site or point-to-site hybrid connectivity on a budget expressroute private circuit through a connectivity provider does not traverse the public internet predictable bandwidth and latency for serious hybrid workloads vnet peering connects two vnets over the microsoft backbone non-transitive by default hub-and-spoke designs; use a hub firewall or virtual wan for transit option copies survives read from secondary? typical use lrs 3 in one datacenter disk / rack failure \u2014 dev/test, data you can recreate zrs 3 across availability zones one datacenter failing \u2014 production default in zone-enabled regions grs / ra-grs lrs + 3 async copies in the paired region regional outage (after failover) ra- variant only backups, dr copies gzrs / ra-gzrs zrs + 3 async copies in the paired region zone and regional failure ra- variant only critical production data blob access tiers are a separate axis from redundancy: hot \u2192 cool (30-day minimum) \u2192 cold (90-day) \u2192 archive (180-day, offline, hours to rehydrate). lifecycle management policies move data between tiers automatically."}, {"id": "patterns", "url": "patterns.html", "title": "How it fits together", "text": "chapter 5 how it fits together four shapes cover most real-world azure systems. each flow reads top to bottom, with every service named. pattern a secure web app with a database flowchart lr u[users] --> fd[front door + waf] fd -->|private link| app[app service / container apps] app -->|managed identity| kv[key vault] app -->|private endpoint| sql[(azure sql)] app -->|private endpoint| st[(blob storage)] app -.telemetry.-> ai[application insights] ai --> la[log analytics] dns points at front door , which terminates tls at the edge, applies waf rules and caches static content. front door reaches the app privately. the app has public access disabled. the app's managed identity fetches secrets from key vault and authenticates to sql and storage with entra tokens, so there are no connection-string passwords. outbound calls go through vnet integration to private endpoints ; private dns zones resolve the service names to private ips. application insights collects requests, dependencies and exceptions into a log analytics workspace. pattern b hub-and-spoke network (landing zone) flowchart tb op[on-premises] ---|expressroute / vpn| gw subgraph hub[hub vnet - connectivity subscription] gw[vpn / er gateway] fw[azure firewall] bas[bastion] dns[dns private resolver] end subgraph s1[spoke: app a prod] a1[app subnet] d1[data subnet] end subgraph s2[spoke: app b prod] a2[aks subnet] end hub |peering| s1 hub |peering| s2 a1 -.udr 0.0.0.0/0.-> fw a2 -.udr 0.0.0.0/0.-> fw a central hub vnet holds shared services: gateways to on-prem, azure firewall , bastion for admin access, and dns. each workload gets its own spoke vnet, usually in its own subscription, peered to the hub. user-defined routes send spoke traffic through the firewall, so spoke-to-spoke and internet egress is inspected centrally. nsgs filter traffic at the subnet or nic level; the firewall does centralized l3\u2013l7 filtering. virtual wan is the managed alternative when you have many regions or branches. pattern c event-driven processing flowchart lr up[client upload] --> blob[(blob storage)] blob -->|blobcreated| eg[event grid] eg --> fn[function: validate] fn -->|command| sb[service bus queue] sb --> w[container apps job: process] w --> cos[(cosmos db)] cos -->|change feed| fn2[function: notify] sb -.failures.-> dlq[dead-letter queue] a file lands in blob storage ; storage publishes a blobcreated event to event grid . a function validates it and drops a durable command on a service bus queue. a worker (container apps job, scaled by keda on queue length) does the heavy lifting and writes to cosmos db . the cosmos change feed triggers downstream reactions. failed messages go to the dead-letter queue for inspection. pattern d data and ai platform flowchart lr src[apps, saas, on-prem dbs] --> adf[data factory / fabric pipelines] iot[devices, logs] --> eh[event hubs] adf --> lake[(onelake / adls gen2)] eh --> lake lake --> spark[databricks / fabric spark] spark --> wh[warehouse / lakehouse] wh --> pbi[power bi] lake --> srch[ai search index] srch --> foundry[foundry: models + agents] foundry --> app2[chat / copilot app] pur[purview] -.catalog + lineage.-> lake batch sources come in through data factory or fabric pipelines; streams come in through event hubs . everything lands in a data lake ( adls gen2 or fabric's onelake ) in open delta/parquet formats, usually in bronze/silver/gold layers. spark (databricks or fabric) cleans and models the data; power bi reports on it. for generative ai, documents are chunked and embedded into ai search ; an app in microsoft foundry retrieves from it and calls a model (retrieval-augmented generation). purview catalogs data, tracks lineage and applies sensitivity labels."}, {"id": "tooling", "url": "tooling.html", "title": "Tooling & IaC", "text": "chapter 6 tooling & infrastructure as code the portal is for exploring. real environments are built with code and pipelines: the cli, an iac language and a ci/cd system. tool what it is when you use it azure portal web ui at portal.azure.com exploring, troubleshooting, one-off checks. every blade's \"export template\" and json view teach you the underlying resource shape. azure cli ( az ) cross-platform command line scripting, quick queries, ci pipelines. --query uses jmespath to filter output. azure powershell ( az ) powershell module windows-heavy shops, object-pipeline scripting cloud shell browser shell with cli, powershell, terraform, kubectl preinstalled when you cannot install tools locally bicep microsoft's declarative iac language; compiles to arm json azure-only shops. day-zero support for new resource types, no state file to manage. arm templates the json format arm natively understands you will read them more than write them; bicep replaced hand-written arm. terraform ( azurerm , azapi ) hashicorp's multi-cloud iac multi-cloud teams, existing terraform expertise. keep state in a locked storage account backend. azure verified modules (avm) microsoft-maintained bicep and terraform modules starting points that follow well-architected defaults azure developer cli ( azd ) app-centric workflow: azd up provisions infra + deploys code from templates spinning up full sample apps quickly; developer inner loop azure devops boards, repos, pipelines (yaml), artifacts, test plans many enterprises run ci/cd here. key pieces: yaml pipelines, service connections, environments and approvals. github actions ci/cd in github with azure/login via oidc federation modern default for new projects; no stored client secrets azure resource graph kql query engine across all your subscriptions' resources inventory questions: \"every public ip in the company\" vs code + azure extensions bicep language server, functions, resources explorers daily editing; the bicep extension's intellisense is the fastest way to discover resource properties azure cli essentials copy # sign in and pick a subscription az login az account list -o table az account set --subscription \"my sub\" # resource groups and inventory az group create -n rg-demo-dev -l eastus2 az resource list -g rg-demo-dev -o table az resource list --query \"[?type=='microsoft.web/sites'].name\" -o tsv # deploy bicep (what-if previews the diff) az deployment group what-if -g rg-demo-dev -f main.bicep az deployment group create -g rg-demo-dev -f main.bicep -p env=dev # rbac az role assignment create --assignee <objectid> \\ --role \"storage blob data reader\" --scope <storageaccountid> az role assignment list --assignee <objectid> --all -o table # everyday service commands az webapp log tail -n app-demo -g rg-demo-dev az aks get-credentials -n aks-demo -g rg-demo-dev az keyvault secret show --vault-name kv-demo -n dbpassword az group delete -n rg-demo-dev --yes --no-wait bicep: storage + managed identity access copy param location string = resourcegroup().location param env string param appprincipalid string resource st 'microsoft.storage/storageaccounts@2023-05-01' = { name: 'stdemo${env}${uniquestring(resourcegroup().id)}' location: location sku: { name: env == 'prod' ? 'standard_zrs' : 'standard_lrs' } kind: 'storagev2' properties: { allowblobpublicaccess: false minimumtlsversion: 'tls1_2' allowsharedkeyaccess: false // force entra auth } } // storage blob data reader (built-in role id) var blobreader = '2a2b9908-6ea1-4ae2-8e65-a410df84e7d1' resource ra 'microsoft.authorization/roleassignments@2022-04-01' = { scope: st name: guid(st.id, appprincipalid, blobreader) properties: { principalid: appprincipalid principaltype: 'serviceprincipal' roledefinitionid: subscriptionresourceid( 'microsoft.authorization/roledefinitions', blobreader) } } output blobendpoint string = st.properties.primaryendpoints.blob naming convention: most teams use the cloud adoption framework pattern <type>-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<nnn> , e.g. rg-payments-prod-eus2-001 , kv-payments-prod-eus2 . some resources (storage accounts, acr) allow only lowercase letters and digits and must be globally unique. ask your team what convention they use before creating anything."}, {"id": "dev", "url": "dev.html", "title": "Building apps", "text": "developer chapter 7 building apps on azure what changes when your code runs in azure: how it authenticates, where config lives, how you run things locally, how it ships, and how it stays observable. examples are in python; every pattern has a direct equivalent in .net, javascript and java. 1. how your code talks to azure services use the official azure sdk packages ( azure-* in python, azure.* in .net, @azure/* in javascript). authenticate with defaultazurecredential . on your laptop it uses your az login session; in azure it uses the app's managed identity. the same code works in both places, with no keys anywhere. storage + key vault with no secrets copy # pip install azure-identity azure-storage-blob azure-keyvault-secrets from azure.identity import defaultazurecredential from azure.storage.blob import blobserviceclient from azure.keyvault.secrets import secretclient # az login locally, managed identity in azure cred = defaultazurecredential() blobs = blobserviceclient( \"https://stdemodev.blob.core.windows.net\", credential=cred) uploads = blobs.get_container_client(\"uploads\") uploads.upload_blob(\"hello.txt\", b\"hi\", overwrite=true) secrets = secretclient( \"https://kv-demo-dev.vault.azure.net\", credential=cred) api_key = secrets.get_secret(\"thirdpartyapikey\").value service bus send + receive copy # pip install azure-servicebus azure-identity from azure.identity import defaultazurecredential from azure.servicebus import servicebusclient, servicebusmessage ns = \"sb-demo-dev.servicebus.windows.net\" with servicebusclient(ns, defaultazurecredential()) as sb: with sb.get_queue_sender(\"orders\") as sender: sender.send_messages(servicebusmessage('{\"orderid\": 123}')) with sb.get_queue_receiver(\"orders\", max_wait_time=5) as rx: for msg in rx: print(str(msg)) rx.complete_message(msg) # or abandon / dead-letter the most common auth error: authorizationpermissionmismatch or 403 while using defaultazurecredential. your identity can sign in but has no data-plane role. ask for (or assign) the right one, e.g. storage blob data contributor , key vault secrets user , azure service bus data sender . role assignments can take a few minutes to apply. 2. the local development loop tool what it gives you tip azure cli login your identity for defaultazurecredential on your laptop run az login then az account show to confirm the right tenant and subscription azurite local emulator for blob, queue and table storage runs as an npm package, docker image or vs code extension. uses usedevelopmentstorage=true functions core tools func start runs functions locally with real triggers local settings live in local.settings.json . keep it out of git. cosmos db emulator local cosmos db for nosql docker image on linux/macos service bus / event hubs emulators local brokers in docker good for unit and integration tests; not every feature is supported sql server in docker local relational database matching azure sql's engine test migrations here before running them against azure sql dev tunnels public https url that forwards to your localhost testing webhooks from event grid or third parties azd azd up provisions infra and deploys code; azd down deletes it great for spinning up a personal dev environment from a template 3. configuration and secrets app settings are environment variables in app service, functions and container apps, the settings you configure show up in your code as environment variables, e.g. os.environ[\"queue_name\"] . keep non-secret config here. key vault references point an app setting at a secret and the platform fetches it with the app's managed identity: @microsoft.keyvault(vaultname=kv-demo;secretname=dbpassword) your code still just reads an env var. app configuration for config shared across many services, or feature flags you want to flip without redeploying. it can hold key vault references too. best of all: no secret at all azure sql, storage, service bus, cosmos db, event hubs, key vault and azure openai all accept entra tokens. if a service supports managed identity, use it and skip the connection-string password. 4. shipping code target how code gets there safe-release feature app service zip deploy, github actions azure/webapps-deploy , azure pipelines task, or a container image deployment slots : deploy to staging , warm up, then swap to production. swap back to roll back. functions func azure functionapp publish , actions, pipelines, or a container slots on premium/dedicated plans container apps push an image to acr, then update the app's image tag revisions with traffic splitting, e.g. 90/10 canary aks image to acr, then helm charts or manifests via a pipeline or gitops (flux) rolling updates, readiness probes, blue/green via ingress static web apps github/azure devops integration builds on every push automatic preview environments per pull request github actions: deploy infra + app with oidc (no stored passwords) copy name: deploy on: push: branches: [main] permissions: id-token: write # lets the job request an oidc token from github contents: read jobs: deploy: runs-on: ubuntu-latest environment: prod # add required reviewers on this environment in github steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: azure/login@v2 with: client-id: ${{ vars.azure_client_id }} # app registration with a federated credential tenant-id: ${{ vars.azure_tenant_id }} subscription-id: ${{ vars.azure_subscription_id }} - name: preview infra changes run: az deployment group what-if -g rg-demo-prod -f infra/main.bicep -p infra/main.prod.bicepparam - name: deploy infra run: az deployment group create -g rg-demo-prod -f infra/main.bicep -p infra/main.prod.bicepparam - uses: azure/webapps-deploy@v3 with: app-name: app-demo-prod slot-name: staging package: . 5. making code cloud-ready expect transient failures networks blip and services throttle (http 429 , 503 ). azure sdks retry with backoff by default; don't wrap them in a second retry loop. set timeouts on every outbound call. make message handlers idempotent : the same message may arrive twice. be stateless instances get added, removed and restarted. keep sessions in redis or a database, files in blob storage, never on local disk. expose a /health endpoint and wire it to the platform's health check or probes. instrument from day one add the azure monitor opentelemetry distro and you get requests, dependencies, exceptions and logs in application insights: from azure.monitor.opentelemetry import configure_azure_monitor configure_azure_monitor() it reads applicationinsights_connection_string from the environment. signing in your users workforce apps : register the app in entra id and use msal , or turn on app service's built-in auth (\"easy auth\") with no code. customer-facing apps : entra external id. apis validate the token's audience and scopes; api management can do this at the gateway. developer gotchas works locally, 403 in azure locally you used your own identity; in azure the app's managed identity is used, and it lacks the role. assign the role to the managed identity. app can't reach the database after networking was locked down public access was disabled. the app needs vnet integration plus a private endpoint, and dns must resolve to the private ip. settings changes restart the app changing app service app settings restarts it. use slots or app configuration for live changes. functions run slowly at first cold start on consumption plans. use flex consumption's always-ready instances or a premium plan for latency-sensitive endpoints. cosmos db bill is surprisingly high usually cross-partition queries or a hot partition key. check ru charge per query in the response headers. time zones are wrong azure services run in utc. store and compare times in utc; convert only for display."}, {"id": "infra", "url": "infra.html", "title": "Running the platform", "text": "infrastructure chapter 8 running the platform the infrastructure side: planning networks, structuring iac, promoting through environments, troubleshooting connectivity and keeping costs and patches under control. what the work usually is provisioning subscriptions, networks and shared services from code reviewing pull requests to iac repos and pipelines granting and auditing access (rbac, pim requests) \"why can't x reach y?\" network tickets patching, backups, dr tests cost reviews and cleanup responding to alerts and defender for cloud findings environments and promotion separate subscriptions (or at least resource groups) for dev, test and prod, so a mistake in dev can't touch prod and costs stay visible. the same iac deploys every environment; only parameter files differ. pipelines promote dev \u2192 test \u2192 prod with what-if output and an approval gate before prod. nobody clicks changes into prod by hand. if they must in an emergency, fix the code afterward so the next deploy doesn't undo it (drift). 1. ip address planning address ranges are painful to change later and must never overlap with on-premises networks or other vnets you might peer with. a small example plan: vnet / subnet range usable ips notes vnet-hub-prod 10.0.0.0/22 1,019 shared connectivity services \u00b7 gatewaysubnet 10.0.0.0/27 27 name is required exactly; vpn/expressroute gateways \u00b7 azurefirewallsubnet 10.0.0.64/26 59 required name and minimum /26 \u00b7 azurebastionsubnet 10.0.0.128/26 59 required name and minimum /26 vnet-app-prod 10.1.0.0/22 1,019 one workload spoke \u00b7 snet-app 10.1.0.0/26 59 delegated to app service for vnet integration \u00b7 snet-pe 10.1.0.64/26 59 private endpoints for sql, storage, key vault vnet-aks-prod 10.2.0.0/20 4,091 kubernetes needs room to grow; overlay networking keeps pod ips out of the vnet azure reserves 5 addresses in every subnet (the first four and the last), so a /27 gives 27 usable, not 32. leave gaps between ranges for growth. 2. structuring infrastructure code bicep: project layout + module wiring copy infra/ main.bicep // composes the modules main.dev.bicepparam // per-environment values main.prod.bicepparam modules/ network.bicep app.bicep data.bicep monitoring.bicep // main.prod.bicepparam using './main.bicep' param env = 'prod' param location = 'eastus2' param appskuname = 'p1v3' // main.bicep param env string param location string param appskuname string module net 'modules/network.bicep' = { name: 'network' params: { env: env location: location addressprefix: '10.1.0.0/22' } } module app 'modules/app.bicep' = { name: 'app' params: { env: env location: location skuname: appskuname subnetid: net.outputs.appsubnetid // outputs flow between modules } } terraform: remote state + first resource copy terraform { required_providers { azurerm = { source = \"hashicorp/azurerm\" version = \"~> 4.0\" } } # state lives in a locked-down storage account backend \"azurerm\" { resource_group_name = \"rg-tfstate\" storage_account_name = \"sttfstate001\" container_name = \"tfstate\" key = \"app-demo/prod.tfstate\" use_azuread_auth = true } } provider \"azurerm\" { features {} # v4 needs a subscription: set here or via arm_subscription_id } resource \"azurerm_resource_group\" \"rg\" { name = \"rg-demo-prod-eus2\" location = \"eastus2\" tags = { env = \"prod\" owner = \"platform\" } } # terraform init \u2192 plan \u2192 apply bicep or terraform? use whatever your team already uses. the concepts (modules, parameters, outputs, plan/what-if, environments) carry over directly. bicep keeps no state file and gets new azure features first. terraform covers other clouds and saas, but you have to manage its state. 3. troubleshooting \"x can't reach y\" dns. does the name resolve, and to the right ip? for a private endpoint you should get a 10.x address, not a public one. most private-networking problems are dns problems. port reachable? test tcp from the source: nc -vz host 1433 or test-netconnection host -port 1433 . nsgs. check effective security rules on the source and destination nics or subnets. routes. check effective routes; a udr may send traffic to a firewall that drops it. firewall. search azure firewall logs for the source ip. service firewall. the paas resource's own \"networking\" settings may block public access or not list your subnet. identity. if you connect but get 401/403, it's rbac, not networking. commands for each step copy # 1 dns: expect a private 10.x ip for private endpoints nslookup stdemoprod.blob.core.windows.net # 2 tcp reachability from the source machine nc -vz sql-demo-prod.database.windows.net 1433 # 3 effective nsg rules on a vm's nic az network nic list-effective-nsg -g rg-app-prod -n nic-vm01 # 4 effective routes on that nic az network nic show-effective-route-table \\ -g rg-app-prod -n nic-vm01 -o table # network watcher end-to-end check az network watcher test-connectivity -g rg-app-prod \\ --source-resource vm01 \\ --dest-address sql-demo-prod.database.windows.net --dest-port 1433 4. cost and hygiene pay less for the same thing reservations (1 or 3 years) for steady vms, sql and cosmos db savings plans for compute that changes shape spot vms for interruptible batch work azure hybrid benefit if you own windows server or sql licenses auto-shutdown and scale-to-zero for dev environments resource graph: find waste copy // unattached managed disks (still billed) resources | where type == \"microsoft.compute/disks\" | where properties.diskstate == \"unattached\" | project name, resourcegroup, sku.name, properties.disksizegb // public ips not attached to anything resources | where type == \"microsoft.network/publicipaddresses\" | where isnull(properties.ipconfiguration) and isnull(properties.natgateway) | project name, resourcegroup, location // resources missing an owner tag resources | where isnull(tags.owner) | summarize count() by type 5. keeping production healthy area baseline to aim for azure tools availability zone-redundant skus for production databases, gateways, app service plans and aks availability zones, zone-redundant storage backup every stateful resource has a backup policy, and restores are tested on a schedule azure backup, sql automated backups, blob soft delete and versioning disaster recovery rto/rpo agreed with app owners; a documented and rehearsed failover site recovery, sql failover groups, cosmos multi-region, front door patching vm os patches on a schedule with maintenance windows; aks on an upgrade channel update manager, aks auto-upgrade, node image upgrades access no standing owner on prod; admins use pim; break-glass accounts monitored pim, access reviews, activity log alerts visibility diagnostic settings on everything, enforced by policy, sent to a central workspace azure policy (deployifnotexists), log analytics, defender for cloud"}, {"id": "containers", "url": "containers.html", "title": "Containers & Kubernetes", "text": "developer infrastructure chapter 9 containers & kubernetes containers show up in almost every azure team, whether apps run on container apps, app service or aks. this chapter covers the vocabulary, the build-and-push loop, and enough kubernetes to read your team's manifests. the vocabulary term what it means where it lives in azure image a packaged app plus everything it needs to run, built from a dockerfile stored in azure container registry (acr) registry a store of images, addressed as registry/repo:tag myacr.azurecr.io/orders-api:1.4.2 container a running instance of an image container apps replica, app service instance, aks pod pod kubernetes' smallest unit: one or more containers sharing a network address aks deployment keeps n copies of a pod running and rolls out new versions aks (container apps does this for you with revisions) service a stable internal address that load-balances across pods aks; type: loadbalancer creates an azure load balancer ingress / gateway http routing from outside the cluster to services app routing add-on (nginx), application gateway for containers namespace a folder inside a cluster for separating teams or apps aks configmap / secret config and sensitive values injected into pods prefer key vault via the secrets store csi driver node pool the vms that run pods; system pools run cluster services, user pools run your apps vm scale sets managed by aks helm chart a templated, versioned package of kubernetes manifests often stored in acr as oci artifacts workload identity lets a pod use a managed identity to call azure services with no secrets aks with oidc issuer enabled dockerfile for a small flask api copy from python:3.12-slim workdir /app copy requirements.txt . run pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt copy . . # run as a non-root user run useradd -m appuser user appuser expose 8000 cmd [\"gunicorn\", \"-b\", \"0.0.0.0:8000\", \"app:app\"] build in the cloud, push to acr copy # create a registry (name must be globally unique, letters/digits) az acr create -g rg-demo-dev -n acrdemodev$random --sku basic # build remotely with acr tasks: no docker needed locally az acr build -r <acrname> -t orders-api:1.0.0 . # list what's in the registry az acr repository list -n <acrname> -o table az acr repository show-tags -n <acrname> --repository orders-api # or build locally and push az acr login -n <acrname> docker build -t <acrname>.azurecr.io/orders-api:1.0.0 . docker push <acrname>.azurecr.io/orders-api:1.0.0 reading a kubernetes manifest deployment.yaml copy apiversion: apps/v1 kind: deployment metadata: name: orders-api namespace: orders spec: replicas: 3 # keep 3 pods running selector: matchlabels: { app: orders-api } template: metadata: labels: app: orders-api azure.workload.identity/use: \"true\" spec: serviceaccountname: orders-api # linked to a managed identity containers: - name: api image: acrdemodev.azurecr.io/orders-api:1.0.0 ports: [{ containerport: 8000 }] resources: requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128mi } limits: { memory: 256mi } readinessprobe: httpget: { path: /health, port: 8000 } --- apiversion: v1 kind: service metadata: name: orders-api namespace: orders spec: selector: { app: orders-api } ports: [{ port: 80, targetport: 8000 }] aks + kubectl essentials copy # small lab cluster that can pull from your registry az aks create -g rg-demo-dev -n aks-demo \\ --node-count 1 --node-vm-size standard_b2s \\ --attach-acr <acrname> \\ --enable-oidc-issuer --enable-workload-identity \\ --generate-ssh-keys # point kubectl at it az aks install-cli # if kubectl isn't installed az aks get-credentials -g rg-demo-dev -n aks-demo # apply and inspect kubectl create namespace orders kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml kubectl get pods -n orders -o wide kubectl describe pod <pod> -n orders # events explain most failures kubectl logs deploy/orders-api -n orders --tail=50 kubectl port-forward svc/orders-api 8080:80 -n orders # roll out a new version and watch it kubectl set image deploy/orders-api api=<acr>.azurecr.io/orders-api:1.0.1 -n orders kubectl rollout status deploy/orders-api -n orders kubectl rollout undo deploy/orders-api -n orders # stop paying for the lab az aks stop -g rg-demo-dev -n aks-demo pod troubleshooting cheat sheet imagepullbackoff wrong image name or tag, or the cluster can't authenticate to acr. check the tag exists and run az aks check-acr -g <rg> -n <aks> --acr <acr>.azurecr.io . crashloopbackoff the container starts then exits. read kubectl logs <pod> --previous ; usually a missing env var or a bad startup command. pending no node has room. kubectl describe pod shows \"insufficient cpu/memory\". lower requests or scale the node pool. oomkilled the container used more memory than its limit. raise the limit or fix the leak. running but not receiving traffic the readiness probe is failing, or the service's selector doesn't match the pod labels. check kubectl get endpoints . 403 calling azure from a pod workload identity isn't wired up: the service account annotation, the federated credential on the managed identity, or the role assignment is missing. same app, less platform: everything above maps onto container apps without managing a cluster. a deployment becomes a container app, a rollout becomes a revision, and hpa/keda scaling becomes a scale rule. the kubernetes terms still matter, because container apps' docs and error messages use them."}, {"id": "ops", "url": "ops.html", "title": "Monitoring & ops", "text": "chapter 10 monitoring & operations azure monitor is the umbrella. underneath it, nearly everything ends up as rows in a log analytics workspace that you query with kql. the telemetry pipeline platform metrics : numeric time series every resource emits for free (cpu, requests, dtu). 93-day retention. activity log : who did what at the control plane: \"user x deleted vm y\". subscription-level. resource logs : data-plane detail (e.g. key vault access, firewall hits). off by default; turn on with diagnostic settings . azure monitor agent + data collection rules : collect vm guest os logs and perf counters. application insights : apm for your code, with opentelemetry support. acting on it alerts : metric, log-search, activity-log and service-health rules. action groups : who to notify and what to trigger (email, sms, webhook, logic app, itsm). workbooks and azure managed grafana : dashboards. service health : azure's own incidents and planned maintenance affecting your resources. advisor : recommendations on cost, security, reliability and performance. keeping things running azure backup : vms, sql in vms, files, blobs, aks, into recovery services or backup vaults. site recovery : replicate vms to another region and fail over. update manager : os patching for azure and arc-enabled machines. network watcher : connection troubleshoot, ip flow verify, flow logs. resource health : is this resource healthy right now? everyday kql copy // failed requests by operation, last 24h (application insights) apprequests | where timegenerated > ago(24h) and success == false | summarize failures = count() by operationname, resultcode | order by failures desc // who deleted or changed things (activity log) azureactivity | where timegenerated > ago(7d) | where operationnamevalue endswith \"delete\" or operationnamevalue endswith \"write\" | project timegenerated, caller, operationnamevalue, resourcegroup, _resourceid | order by timegenerated desc // p95 latency in 5-minute buckets apprequests | where timegenerated > ago(6h) | summarize p95 = percentile(durationms, 95) by bin(timegenerated, 5m) | render timechart // resource graph: every storage account that still allows public blob access resources | where type == \"microsoft.storage/storageaccounts\" | where properties.allowblobpublicaccess == true | project name, resourcegroup, subscriptionid, location"}, {"id": "waf", "url": "waf.html", "title": "Well-Architected", "text": "chapter 11 the well-architected framework microsoft's design-review vocabulary. expect architects to use these five pillars by name, and expect trade-offs between them. pillar the question typical azure answers reliability does it survive failure and recover on time? availability zones, multi-region with front door, geo-replication, backup, health probes, retries with backoff, defined rto/rpo security is access minimal and is data protected? entra id + managed identities, private endpoints, key vault, defender for cloud, waf, encryption with customer-managed keys where required cost optimization are we paying only for value? right-sizing, autoscale, reservations and savings plans, spot vms, storage tiering, budgets, tagging for showback operational excellence can we deploy and run it safely? iac, ci/cd with approvals, monitoring and alerts, runbooks, safe deployment (slots, canary), blameless postmortems performance efficiency does it scale with demand? autoscale rules, caching (redis, front door), partitioning, async messaging, load testing with azure load testing the cloud adoption framework (caf) is the companion for the organization side: strategy, plan, ready (landing zones), adopt (migrate/innovate), govern, secure and manage. the well-architected framework is per-workload; caf is per-organization."}, {"id": "labs", "url": "labs.html", "title": "Walkthroughs", "text": "chapter 12 walkthroughs five end-to-end examples of common setups. each one creates its own resource group so you can delete everything with one command at the end. run them in a bash shell (macos terminal, wsl or cloud shell) after az login . before you start: use a personal or sandbox subscription, not your employer's production one. each walkthrough lists rough cost; most cost cents if you delete them the same day. set loc to a region near you that your subscription allows. 1 \u00b7 web app that reads a key vault secret with managed identity ~45 min \u00b7 b1 plan deploys a flask app to app service with a managed identity that reads a secret from key vault through an app setting, with no password anywhere. step 1: create the resources copy rg=rg-lab1; loc=eastus2; sfx=$random app=app-lab1-$sfx; kv=kv-lab1-$sfx az group create -n $rg -l $loc az appservice plan create -n plan-lab1 -g $rg --sku b1 --is-linux az webapp create -n $app -g $rg -p plan-lab1 --runtime \"python:3.12\" # turn on a system-assigned managed identity az webapp identity assign -n $app -g $rg app_principal=$(az webapp identity show -n $app -g $rg --query principalid -o tsv) # key vault using azure rbac for permissions az keyvault create -n $kv -g $rg -l $loc --enable-rbac-authorization true kv_id=$(az keyvault show -n $kv --query id -o tsv) me=$(az ad signed-in-user show --query id -o tsv) # you can write secrets; the app can only read them az role assignment create --assignee $me --role \"key vault secrets officer\" --scope $kv_id az role assignment create --assignee-object-id $app_principal \\ --assignee-principal-type serviceprincipal \\ --role \"key vault secrets user\" --scope $kv_id # role assignments take a minute or two to apply sleep 60 az keyvault secret set --vault-name $kv -n greeting --value \"hello from key vault\" # app setting that points at the secret az webapp config appsettings set -n $app -g $rg \\ --settings \"greeting=@microsoft.keyvault(vaultname=$kv;secretname=greeting)\" step 2: write and deploy the app copy mkdir lab1 && cd lab1 printf 'flask\\ngunicorn\\n' > requirements.txt cat > app.py <<'py' import os from flask import flask app = flask(__name__) @app.get(\"/\") def index(): return os.environ.get(\"greeting\", \"greeting is not set\") py az webapp up -n $app -g $rg --runtime \"python:3.12\" curl https://$app.azurewebsites.net verify: the page prints \"hello from key vault\". in the portal, open the app \u2192 settings \u2192 environment variables ; the greeting row shows a green key vault reference status. if it shows an error, the role assignment hadn't applied yet: restart the app after a minute. what failure looks like: if the identity loses its role, the app receives the raw @microsoft.keyvault(...) text instead of the secret value. clean up copy az group delete -n $rg --yes --no-wait # key vault soft-deletes; purge it so the name can be reused az keyvault purge -n $kv 2 \u00b7 lock a storage account behind a private endpoint ~40 min \u00b7 b1s vm builds a vnet and puts a storage account behind a private endpoint with public access off. dns resolves to a private ip from inside the vnet but not from your laptop. build it copy rg=rg-lab2; loc=eastus2; st=stlab2$random zone=privatelink.blob.core.windows.net az group create -n $rg -l $loc az network vnet create -g $rg -n vnet-lab2 --address-prefix 10.10.0.0/16 \\ --subnet-name snet-vm --subnet-prefixes 10.10.1.0/24 az network vnet subnet create -g $rg --vnet-name vnet-lab2 \\ -n snet-pe --address-prefixes 10.10.2.0/24 # storage with no public network access az storage account create -n $st -g $rg -l $loc --sku standard_lrs \\ --public-network-access disabled --allow-blob-public-access false st_id=$(az storage account show -n $st -g $rg --query id -o tsv) # private endpoint for the blob service az network private-endpoint create -g $rg -n pe-$st \\ --vnet-name vnet-lab2 --subnet snet-pe \\ --private-connection-resource-id $st_id --group-id blob --connection-name blob-conn # private dns so the normal name resolves to the private ip az network private-dns zone create -g $rg -n $zone az network private-dns link vnet create -g $rg -n link-lab2 -z $zone -v vnet-lab2 -e false az network private-endpoint dns-zone-group create -g $rg --endpoint-name pe-$st \\ -n default --private-dns-zone $zone --zone-name blob # a vm with no public ip to test from az vm create -g $rg -n vm-lab2 --image ubuntu2204 --size standard_b1s \\ --vnet-name vnet-lab2 --subnet snet-vm --public-ip-address \"\" \\ --admin-username azureuser --generate-ssh-keys prove it copy # from your laptop: public answer, and requests are refused nslookup $st.blob.core.windows.net # from inside the vnet: a 10.10.2.x address az vm run-command invoke -g $rg -n vm-lab2 --command-id runshellscript \\ --scripts \"nslookup $st.blob.core.windows.net\" verify: inside the vnet the name resolves through {name}.privatelink.blob.core.windows.net to a 10.10.2.x address. without the dns zone link, the vm resolves the public ip and the connection fails. that's the most common private-endpoint bug. clean up copy az group delete -n $rg --yes --no-wait 3 \u00b7 container apps that scale to zero ~30 min \u00b7 pay per use runs a public container on container apps with an http scale rule: replicas appear under load and drop to zero when idle. deploy and scale copy az extension add --name containerapp --upgrade az provider register -n microsoft.app --wait az provider register -n microsoft.operationalinsights --wait rg=rg-lab3; loc=eastus2 az group create -n $rg -l $loc # creates the environment, log analytics workspace and app in one go az containerapp up -n ca-lab3 -g $rg -l $loc \\ --image mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart:latest \\ --target-port 80 --ingress external # scale between 0 and 5 replicas, 10 concurrent requests each az containerapp update -n ca-lab3 -g $rg \\ --min-replicas 0 --max-replicas 5 \\ --scale-rule-name http-rule --scale-rule-type http \\ --scale-rule-http-concurrency 10 url=https://$(az containerapp show -n ca-lab3 -g $rg \\ --query properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn -o tsv) echo $url generate load and watch copy # terminal 1: send ~500 requests in parallel batches for i in $(seq 1 10); do for j in $(seq 1 50); do curl -s -o /dev/null $url & done; wait done # terminal 2: watch replicas watch -n 5 \"az containerapp replica list -n ca-lab3 -g $rg -o table\" # revisions: each config change makes a new one az containerapp revision list -n ca-lab3 -g $rg -o table verify: replicas climb above 1 under load, then drop to 0 a few minutes after the load stops. the next request after that is slower; that's a cold start. then find the app's logs in its log analytics workspace with containerappconsolelogs_cl | take 20 . clean up copy az group delete -n $rg --yes --no-wait 4 \u00b7 deploy bicep from github actions with no secrets ~45 min \u00b7 storage only creates an entra app that trusts a github repo through oidc, grants it contributor on one resource group, and deploys a bicep file on every push to main . step 1: identity and trust (run once) copy repo=your-github-user/your-repo # change this rg=rg-lab4; loc=eastus2 az group create -n $rg -l $loc app_id=$(az ad app create --display-name gh-lab4-deployer --query appid -o tsv) az ad sp create --id $app_id # least privilege: contributor on this one resource group only az role assignment create --assignee $app_id --role contributor \\ --scope $(az group show -n $rg --query id -o tsv) # trust tokens from pushes to main in your repo az ad app federated-credential create --id $app_id --parameters \"{ \\\"name\\\": \\\"gh-main\\\", \\\"issuer\\\": \\\"https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com\\\", \\\"subject\\\": \\\"repo:$repo:ref:refs/heads/main\\\", \\\"audiences\\\": [\\\"api://azureadtokenexchange\\\"] }\" # store the ids as repo variables (they are not secrets) gh variable set azure_client_id -r $repo -b $app_id gh variable set azure_tenant_id -r $repo -b $(az account show --query tenantid -o tsv) gh variable set azure_subscription_id -r $repo -b $(az account show --query id -o tsv) infra/main.bicep copy param location string = resourcegroup().location resource st 'microsoft.storage/storageaccounts@2023-05-01' = { name: 'stlab4${uniquestring(resourcegroup().id)}' location: location sku: { name: 'standard_lrs' } kind: 'storagev2' properties: { allowblobpublicaccess: false minimumtlsversion: 'tls1_2' } tags: { lab: '4', deployedby: 'github-actions' } } output name string = st.name .github/workflows/deploy.yml copy name: deploy-infra on: push: branches: [main] permissions: id-token: write contents: read jobs: deploy: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: azure/login@v2 with: client-id: ${{ vars.azure_client_id }} tenant-id: ${{ vars.azure_tenant_id }} subscription-id: ${{ vars.azure_subscription_id }} - run: az deployment group what-if -g rg-lab4 -f infra/main.bicep - run: az deployment group create -g rg-lab4 -f infra/main.bicep verify: push to main , open the actions tab, and confirm the what-if step lists the storage account as \"create\". run it again and what-if shows \"nochange\". bicep deployments are idempotent. gotcha: the federated credential's subject must match exactly how the job runs. if you add environment: prod to the job, the subject becomes repo:owner/repo:environment:prod and you need a second credential. the error is aadsts700213: no matching federated identity record found . clean up copy az group delete -n rg-lab4 --yes --no-wait az ad app delete --id $app_id 5 \u00b7 kql against the free demo workspace ~30 min \u00b7 free microsoft hosts a demo log analytics workspace full of sample telemetry. open it from the log analytics tutorial to try these query patterns. five kql patterns copy // 1 look at a table: always start small apprequests | take 10 // 2 filter rows and pick columns apprequests | where timegenerated > ago(1h) and success == false | project timegenerated, name, resultcode, durationms // 3 aggregate apprequests | where timegenerated > ago(1d) | summarize requests = count(), avgms = avg(durationms) by name | top 10 by requests // 4 time series and a chart apprequests | where timegenerated > ago(1d) | summarize count() by bin(timegenerated, 15m), success | render timechart // 5 join requests to the exceptions they caused apprequests | where timegenerated > ago(1d) and success == false | join kind=inner (appexceptions | project operationid, exceptiontype, outermessage) on operationid | summarize count() by name, exceptiontype | order by count_ desc note: each | passes the previous result to the next operator. query 4's shape (filter, bin by time, count) is the same one a log alert rule uses."}, {"id": "onboarding", "url": "onboarding.html", "title": "Mapping an environment", "text": "chapter 13 mapping an environment every azure estate is set up differently. the questions to answer about any environment you work in, and read-only commands that answer some of them directly. access which entra tenant(s) and subscriptions will i work in? how do i request access, and is it through pim (just-in-time)? is there a sandbox subscription where i can experiment? which entra groups should i be in? do i need a separate admin account for privileged work? how things ship where is the infrastructure code: bicep, terraform, or both? azure devops or github? where do pipelines live? what environments exist, and how does a change reach prod? who approves production changes? is there change management? are there internal modules or templates i should reuse? the platform is there a landing zone? who owns the hub network? what naming and tagging conventions do we use? which azure policies will block me (regions, skus, public ips)? which services are approved, and which are off-limits? where do logs go, and which dashboards does on-call use? who owns cost, and are there budgets per team? inventory an environment (read-only) copy # which tenants and subscriptions can i see? az account list --query \"[].{name:name, id:id, tenant:tenantid, default:isdefault}\" -o table # what access do i actually have? az role assignment list --assignee $(az ad signed-in-user show --query id -o tsv) --all \\ --query \"[].{role:roledefinitionname, scope:scope}\" -o table # management group tree (if you can read it) az account management-group list -o table # what's deployed, by type, across everything i can see az graph query -q \"resources | summarize count() by type | order by count_ desc\" --first 50 -o table # which resource groups exist and how are they tagged? az group list --query \"[].{name:name, location:location, tags:tags}\" -o table # which policies apply to this subscription? az policy assignment list --query \"[].{name:displayname, scope:scope}\" -o table working habits keep a personal notes file of resource names, subscription ids and who owns what. keep an up-to-date architecture diagram of each production app you touch. read the last few incident write-ups; they show where the system really breaks. read an app's iac before changing anything in the portal. things to avoid creating resources in a production subscription to \"try something\". changing anything in the portal that is managed by iac; the next deploy will revert it, or worse. regenerating storage or cosmos db keys: every app using the old key breaks. deleting resource groups you didn't create, even if they look abandoned. pasting connection strings, keys or tokens into chat, tickets or ai tools."}, {"id": "links", "url": "links.html", "title": "Docs & links", "text": "chapter 14 docs & links primary sources first. azure renames and retires things often, so trust current microsoft docs over blog posts more than a year old. official docs & guidance azure documentation service docs, quickstarts and api references azure architecture center reference architectures, design patterns, technology choice guides well-architected framework pillars, service guides, assessments cloud adoption framework landing zones, governance, naming and tagging bicep documentation language reference and resource type schemas azure updates previews, ga announcements and retirements pricing calculator estimate before you build tools & samples azure free account starter credit plus always-free service tiers azure developer cli templates deploy full sample apps with azd up azure verified modules production-grade bicep/terraform modules to read and reuse azure-samples on github thousands of runnable samples kql reference pair with the free demo log analytics workspace in the docs subscription and service limits bookmark this; you will hit a quota eventually video & community john savill's technical training deep, clear whiteboard explainers of individual services microsoft azure youtube includes azure friday and conference sessions microsoft tech community product-team blogs, often the first word on changes azure charts community site tracking services, regions and updates r/azure real-world questions and gotchas"}, {"id": "glossary", "url": "glossary.html", "title": "Glossary", "text": "chapter 15 glossary terms and acronyms used across azure docs and this guide."}];
